  "text": "miştir\n\nAssistant: To solve the problem, we need to find the least positive value of \\( t \\) such that the sequence\n\\[ \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha) \\]\nis an arithmetic progression for some \\( \\alpha \\) with \\( 0 < \\alpha < \\pi \\).\n\nFirst, recall the property of the arccos function:\n\\[ \\arccos (\\cos x) = x \\]\nif \\( x \\) is within the range \\([0, \\pi]\\). For \\( x \\) outside this range, the value is given by\n\\[ \\arccos (\\cos x) = 2\\pi - x \\]\nif \\( x \\) is within \\(\\pi, 2\\pi\\), and\n\\[ \\arccos (\\cos x) = - (2\\pi - x) \\]\nif \\( x \\) is within \\(-2\\pi, -\\pi\\).\n\nGiven the sequence \\( \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha), \\ \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha) \\), we assume \\( \\alpha \\) is within the range \\([0, \\pi]\\) so that the arccos function simplifies to:\n\\[ \\arccos (\\cos \\alpha) = \\alpha \\]\n\\[ \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha) = 3\\alpha \\]\n\\[ \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha) = 11\\alpha \\]\n\\[ \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha) = t\\alpha \\]\n\nFor these to be in arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms must be constant. Let's denote this common difference by \\( d \\). Then we have:\n\\[ 3\\alpha - \\alpha = d \\Rightarrow 2\\alpha = d \\]\n\\[ 11\\alpha - 3\\alpha = d \\Rightarrow 8\\alpha = d \\]\n\\[ t\\alpha - 11\\alpha = d \\Rightarrow (t-11)\\alpha = d \\]\n\nFrom the first two equations, we see:\n\\[ 2\\alpha = 8\\alpha \\Rightarrow 2 = 8 \\]\nwhich is a contradiction unless \\( \\alpha = 0 \\) or \\( \\alpha = \\pi \\), but we are given \\( 0 < \\alpha < \\pi \\). Instead, we can solve for \\( t \\) directly from the third equation:\n\\[ (t-11)\\alpha = 2\\alpha \\Rightarrow t - 11 = 2 \\Rightarrow t = 13 \\]\n\nThus, the least positive value of \\( t \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{13} \\]",
